    "problem": "Run the mobile validator ^[6-9]\\d{9}$ (via re.match) on: '9876543210', '1234567890', '98765432100'. Also run re.findall(r'\\w+@\\w+\\.\\w+', 'mail me at a.b@x.com or q@y.in') and state the result.",
    "steps": [
     "'9876543210': first char 9 in [6-9]; remaining '876543210' is exactly 9 digits; $ reached. MATCH.",
     "'1234567890': first char 1 is not in [6-9]. No match - the pattern is anchored, so it cannot slide to a later position.",
     "'98765432100': 11 digits: [6-9] plus \\d{9} accounts for 10 characters, then $ fails on the extra 0. No match.",
     "findall: \\w+ matches word characters; in 'a.b@x.com' the \\w+ before @ can only cover 'b' (the dot is not a word char), so the first match is 'b@x.com'; then 'q@y.in' matches fully. Result: ['b@x.com', 'q@y.in'] - a famous gotcha showing simple email patterns are approximations."
    ],
    "answer": "'9876543210' -> MATCH; '1234567890' -> no match; '98765432100' -> no match. And findall on the sentence returns ['b@x.com', 'q@y.in']."
